# Copy to .env.local at the repository root. .env.local is gitignored; a real
# environment variable beats it, which is what makes CI and launchd work with
# no file at all.
#
# **Every name here begins LINGTAI_** (#63), and the reason is not tidiness.
# A managed project's own environment file is merged over this one, so a project
# whose file was missing a DATABASE_URL line used to get *Lingtai's own log*
# under a name its application connects to without hesitating — and the recipe's
# `env.required` could not tell that apart from a correct line, because the
# merged data had a value either way. No application asks for LINGTAI_*, so a
# missing line is now an absent value, which refuses the run loudly.
#
# A project's own file keeps its own names: DATABASE_URL there stays
# DATABASE_URL, because that is what the project's application reads.

# --- the event log ----------------------------------------------------------
# Two connection strings, one database.
#
# LINGTAI_DATABASE_URL is pooled, for ordinary queries. LINGTAI_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL is session
# mode, for migrations, LISTEN/NOTIFY and advisory locks. A transaction pooler
# breaks all three, and breaks them *without erroring* — a cross-connection
# NOTIFY simply never arrives. See doc/decisions/0009-two-connections.md.
#
# This must be its own database, not one belonging to a managed project.
# Lingtai has to keep running while a managed project is the thing being
# changed.
LINGTAI_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:6543/postgres
LINGTAI_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/postgres

# --- the log the tests write to --------------------------------------------
# A *different* database, and the suite refuses to run without it.
#
# The tests are not mocked: they append real events, run real projections and
# take real advisory locks. Pointed at the operator's own log, they leave work
# items and board cards behind — twenty-four cards from ten throwaway `esctest*`
# projects, in the run that led to this section existing.
#
# Cleaning that up is not cheap either. Truncating a projection and replaying it
# brings the cards straight back, so the only way to remove them is to delete
# from an append-only table, which this system should never make routine.
#
# Give it its schema with:
#   LINGTAI_TEST=1 pnpm db:init && LINGTAI_TEST=1 pnpm db:bootstrap
LINGTAI_TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:6543/postgres
LINGTAI_TEST_DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://user:password@host:5432/postgres

# --- GitHub -----------------------------------------------------------------
# An App, not a personal access token: a fine-grained token can be wrong in a
# way nothing reports. See README "Connecting GitHub" and
# doc/decisions/0006-github-app.md.
LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_ID=
# The private key. A path by default so the key never has to be pasted into a
# shell; `~` is expanded. LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY takes the PEM inline instead,
# for hosts that can only carry it as one line.
LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH=~/.ssh/lingtai-agent.private-key.pem
#LINGTAI_GITHUB_APP_PRIVATE_KEY=

# Optional. Set the same value in the GitHub App's webhook settings, point the
# App at <your tunnel>/api/webhook, and a new issue reaches the daemon in
# seconds instead of waiting for its sweep.
#
# Leaving it unset is fine: the daemon sweeps GitHub on a long interval anyway,
# so a webhook is an optimisation and never a dependency. Without it the route
# answers 503 rather than pretending a delivery landed.
LINGTAI_GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# --- the board ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the board is, for the link a subscriber is handed.
#
# Optional, and `http://localhost:3200` without it. It is worth setting the
# moment a notification is read anywhere but this machine: an extension is given
# the link rather than building one, so a Telegram message about a blocked
# ticket carries whatever this says and nothing can correct it later.
#LINGTAI_BOARD_URL=http://localhost:3200
